## Notes for the reviewer

- `runScheduledJob` (`src/worker/cron.ts`) is today `export async function
  runScheduledJob(_env: Env): Promise<void> {}` — an empty placeholder. Every
  test in `test/cron-sweep.test.ts` is RED for the right reason right now:
  no row is ever claimed, no `fetch` call is ever made (so every `calls`
  assertion fails at `toHaveLength`), and `sentAt` never moves.
- Mocking mechanism mirrors `test/push-test-route.test.ts` exactly:
  `vi.stubGlobal("fetch", ...)` substitutes only the outbound push service's
  HTTP response; `sendPush`'s real sign/encrypt path runs against
  structurally-real P-256 keys generated via `crypto.subtle`, matching the
  existing repo idiom. No mock of the SUT, no mock of a concrete type where
  an interface exists, no chained mocks, no `was_called`-only assertion —
  every assertion pairs a call-count check with an effect check (`sentAt`
  state, `push_subscriptions` row survival).
- Reminders and Tasks are seeded via direct D1 insert (not through
  `/api/reminders` / `/api/tasks`) because the routes do not expose setting
  a past `fireAt`, a pre-set `sentAt`, or a `done`/`missed` Task status
  directly — this is necessary Given-state setup, not an implementation leak;
  every assertion is against the sweep's own observable outputs (dispatch
  call count, `sentAt`, `push_subscriptions` survival), never against an
  internal method name.
- AC-5's first case (`Promise.all([runScheduledJob(env), runScheduledJob(env)])`)
  is the direct encoding of the AC's own "a second runScheduledJob in the
  same window sends nothing" — two overlapping ticks must not double-dispatch
  the same due row.
- No production code was written or modified. `src/worker/cron.ts` is
  untouched by this session.

